From: Local build <local@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] EXP: outputlayer: a cursor move pushed out asynchronously does not wake the render loop (KWIN_ASYNC_CURSOR_MOVES)

A cursor move is committed on its own (the compositor's repaintScheduled handler: presentAsync, a
cursor-plane-only commit) but OutputLayer::scheduleRepaint also woke the render loop, and composite()
then presented an empty frame that claimed the next vblank: a client redrawing in response to the
same pointer move (a VM's window during a drag, ready ~1.8 ms before that vblank, 2026-10-10) could
only take the one after. With the knob (environment or $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kwin-async-cursor-moves), the
cursor layer emits repaintScheduled first and wakes the render loop only when the handler did not
push a pure move out itself (a new cursor image still gets a frame, for its frame callbacks). Every
other source of frames keeps its own scheduleRepaint; screencasts follow the cursor through
Cursors::positionChanged. Off by default. Replaces the withdrawn h16/0027 (skip empty frames).


6.8.1 version (2026-10-20), redesigned: 6.8.1 runs the cursor handler from a 0 ms timer (9503285406), after
scheduleRepaint() has returned, so the handler can no longer tell scheduleRepaint() synchronously that it pushed
the move out. The cursor layer now defers the render loop's wake to a 0 ms timer of its own, started after the
handler's: the handler drops it for a pure move it presented asynchronously, composite() drops it when it handles
the cursor layer in its frame (where 6.8.1 stops the handler's timer), and in every other case - a new image, a
failed asynchronous commit, no handler at all - the timer wakes the render loop with the earliest target asked for.
